WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.679 --> 00:00:04.960
Okay, so first things first, let's take a look at some lighting terminology

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.960
and what types of lights we have available in Unreal Engine 5.

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.960
The topics that we're going to cover in this

4
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.960
are going to be lighting definitions and types,

5
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.960
what the difference is between static lighting

6
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.960
versus dynamic lighting,

7
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.460
as well as what light types are available for us.

8
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:23.269
So, when we go to talk about lighting definitions

9
00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:25.269
and certain terminologies that we'll be using,

10
00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:28.269
and you'll hear me use this a lot as I go through this course,

11
00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:31.070
direct lighting is the light that falls onto a surface

12
00:00:31.149 --> 00:00:33.149
without any interference, so essentially from

13
00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:35.149
the point that the light has been emitted

14
00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:37.149
directly onto the object.

15
00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:39.500
Then we have indirect lighting,

16
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.500
or bounce lighting.

17
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.500
So that's lighting that's been reflected by another surface

18
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.500
that's nearby, and bounces off that surface

19
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.500
onto the object that you're looking at,

20
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.500
carrying some of the color information

21
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.500
from the surface that it's bouncing off.

22
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.500
And then we have the shadows,

23
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.500
which happens essentially when

24
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.500
Unreal Engine takes a snapshot of a mesh

25
00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:01.579
from a lighting's point of view and projects

26
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:03.579
that shadow onto the opposite side of the actor.

27
00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:05.939
So, again, you'll hear me

28
00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:07.939
use the terms direct lighting and direct lighting

29
00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:09.939
quite a lot, so

30
00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:11.939
just keep in mind what these both mean

31
00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:13.939
and understand

32
00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:16.799
how they differ.

33
00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:18.799
When we talk about static versus dynamic lighting,

34
00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:20.799
we have a few options inside of Unreal.

35
00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:22.799
So Lumen

36
00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:24.799
is the new Unreal Engine 5

37
00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:26.799
fully dynamic global

38
00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:28.799
illumination system. It renders the fuse

39
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:30.879
into reflection with infinite bounces

40
00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:32.879
and indirect specular reflections

41
00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:34.879
in large, detailed environments

42
00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:36.879
from really, really small to large

43
00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:38.879
setups.

44
00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:40.879
Then we also have

45
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:42.879
baked or static lighting,

46
00:01:42.879 --> 00:01:44.879
which is pre-computed lighting, similar to what

47
00:01:44.879 --> 00:01:46.879
you'd see with

48
00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:48.879
other rendering engines, such as V-Ray,

49
00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:50.879
Corona, et cetera. It creates

50
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:52.879
high quality indirect lighting

51
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.879
and has no performance

52
00:01:54.879 --> 00:01:56.879
cost, essentially, on

53
00:01:56.959 --> 00:01:58.959
real-time, because everything's

54
00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:00.959
pre-computed and stored in textures.

55
00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:03.310
And then we have real-time

56
00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.310
dynamic lighting, which is lighting that's

57
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.310
updated on each frame. It's fully dynamic

58
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.310
and can be moved around and modified at runtime.

59
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.310
It has no global illumination,

60
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.310
so there's only direct lighting, so no

61
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.310
indirect lighting

62
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.310
whatsoever, unlike Lumen.

63
00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.300
But it's also very expensive to use

64
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.300
and it does affect real-time

65
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.719
performance.

66
00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:26.719
Unreal Engine can use a hybrid

67
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.800
between both static and dynamic lighting,

68
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.800
so that's something to keep

69
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.990
in mind.

70
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.990
We have also ray-traced

71
00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.990
lighting. This type of lighting

72
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.990
is real-time global illumination,

73
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.990
updated with all lights set to

74
00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.990
movable. It's important to note that

75
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.990
some aspects of

76
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.990
ray tracing are taken into account with

77
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.990
Lumen, such as reflections and shadowing when needed.

78
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.990
But the important thing is

79
00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:52.990
the difference between Lumen and ray-traced

80
00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.990
lighting is that ray-trace

81
00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:57.069
actually is hardware ray-tracing, which means

82
00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:59.069
that you need a graphics card able to

83
00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:01.069
support it, whereas Lumen doesn't necessarily

84
00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:03.069
need that. It's software ray-traced,

85
00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:05.259
so it does not actually need

86
00:03:05.259 --> 00:03:07.259
a card, for example, like an RTX

87
00:03:07.259 --> 00:03:09.259
Nvidia card, to

88
00:03:09.259 --> 00:03:11.259
do its global

89
00:03:11.259 --> 00:03:13.650
illumination calculations.

90
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.650
The light types that we're going to see, and I'm running through this

91
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.650
as I go here, but we'll be doing an exercise

92
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.650
later on so you can actually see each light

93
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.650
and how they all differ.

94
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.650
We have directional light, like a sun,

95
00:03:23.729 --> 00:03:25.729
a point light, a spotlight,

96
00:03:25.729 --> 00:03:27.729
rectangular light, a skylight,

97
00:03:27.729 --> 00:03:29.729
and then a couple of extra light

98
00:03:29.729 --> 00:03:31.729
setups here, which are called the HDRI Backdrop

99
00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:33.729
and Sun and Sky, which essentially are a couple

100
00:03:33.729 --> 00:03:35.729
of blueprints that are set up

101
00:03:35.729 --> 00:03:37.729
for HDR lighting in the case of

102
00:03:37.729 --> 00:03:39.729
HDRI Backdrops, and Sun and Sky

103
00:03:39.729 --> 00:03:41.729
allows you to essentially

104
00:03:41.729 --> 00:03:43.729
have a very accurate

105
00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:45.729
sun positioning for a particular

106
00:03:45.729 --> 00:03:47.729
latitude and longitude in a specific place.

107
00:03:47.729 --> 00:03:49.729
Really nice

108
00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.729
couple of blueprints that you can use there specifically

109
00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.810
if you need them, or that sort of thing for your project.

110
00:03:54.969 --> 00:03:56.969
Now, when it comes to the properties and settings of the

111
00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.969
lights, we're going

112
00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:00.969
to cover the light properties and the

113
00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:03.349
mobility options for each light.

114
00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:05.349
So, when it comes to the properties and settings

115
00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:07.349
that we can change, for

116
00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:09.349
point lights and spotlights,

117
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.349
we are looking at candelas,

118
00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:13.349
lumens, or unitless setups

119
00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:15.349
for the intensity.

120
00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:17.350
Now, importantly, 100 watts on a light bulb

121
00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.769
would be called 100 lumens.

122
00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:21.769
However, the directional light, which is more like a

123
00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.850
sun, is calculated in lux values.

124
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.850
In each one of the lights,

125
00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:27.850
all these things are going to be common,

126
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.850
so we're going to have

127
00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:31.980
the intensity,

128
00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.980
indirect lighting intensity, so how much they

129
00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.980
contribute to that indirect bounce lighting.

130
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.980
The attenuation, which is how

131
00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.980
far from the emission

132
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.980
point of the light the light actually travels.

133
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.069
We can use

134
00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:46.069
temperature if we want to, to change the color

135
00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:48.069
without having to actually change the color

136
00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:50.839
and use a temperature value.

137
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.920
Source radius and source angle.

138
00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.920
Source angles you'll see

139
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.920
on the directional light and source radius you'll

140
00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.920
see on every other type of light, which will allow you

141
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.920
to change the softness of the radius as well as

142
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.920
the size of the specular

143
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.920
reflection on the surfaces.

144
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.920
Whether to use

145
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.920
inverse squared falloff for lighting,

146
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.920
and this is something I recommend to just use

147
00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.920
for everything since it's a more realistic

148
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.920
approach to set up lighting.

149
00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.050
And then we also have IES profiles

150
00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.050
if we want to actually change the

151
00:05:19.129 --> 00:05:21.129
way that light is distributed based on

152
00:05:21.129 --> 00:05:23.129
a particular

153
00:05:23.129 --> 00:05:25.290
real-life profile.

154
00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.379
When it comes to light mobility,

155
00:05:27.379 --> 00:05:29.379
we have static, which essentially means

156
00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:31.379
this light's going to be baked

157
00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:33.379
indirect and direct lighting.

158
00:05:33.379 --> 00:05:35.379
It's the cheapest to use once again as it gets

159
00:05:35.379 --> 00:05:37.379
stored into textures and does not

160
00:05:37.379 --> 00:05:39.379
have an impact in performance at real-time.

161
00:05:39.379 --> 00:05:42.019
We've got stationary,

162
00:05:42.019 --> 00:05:44.250
which is baked indirect lighting.

163
00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.250
It's high quality

164
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.600
direct lighting

165
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.680
and shadows that are on the fly, essentially that's the

166
00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.680
dynamic side of it.

167
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.680
But it has a limited use. You can only have a

168
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.680
maximum of four overlapping lights,

169
00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.680
but it's the highest quality to

170
00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.680
medium performance if you're using a static

171
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.680
lighting sort of set.

172
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.019
And then we have movable,

173
00:06:06.019 --> 00:06:08.019
which is fully dynamic lights and shadows.

174
00:06:08.019 --> 00:06:10.019
If you're using Lumen, you will get

175
00:06:10.019 --> 00:06:12.019
indirect lighting out of this.

176
00:06:12.019 --> 00:06:14.019
If you're not,

177
00:06:14.019 --> 00:06:16.250
you will only get direct lighting,

178
00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.250
as I mentioned earlier.

179
00:06:18.329 --> 00:06:20.329
It has the highest performance cost.

180
00:06:20.329 --> 00:06:22.329
And again, if you're not using Lumen, you won't

181
00:06:22.329 --> 00:06:24.329
get indirect lighting, but again,

182
00:06:24.329 --> 00:06:26.329
if you're using Lumen, you will.

183
00:06:26.329 --> 00:06:28.329
That's really it. After this, we're just going to

184
00:06:28.329 --> 00:06:30.329
get into actually taking a look at Lumen

185
00:06:30.329 --> 00:06:32.329
as the premier

186
00:06:32.329 --> 00:06:34.329
Unreal Engine 5 lighting system.

187
00:06:34.329 --> 00:06:36.329
Let's go ahead and do that.
